 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "ilL1lF64AAAJ",
   "from": "Dorota Muth",
   "author": "6b213090d99bc938",
   "date": "2016-09-08T09:35:48Z",
   "subject": "multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">Dear MsStats Team,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">I run label-free SRM experiment for 108\r\nproteins in two conditions (wild type and mutant) with 4 biological replicates\r\neach (replicates 1-8); the 108 targets were separated in two transition lists\r\nin such a way that some peptides from the same protein were registered in two different\r\nruns. I included the same housekeeping endogenous peptides in every run so list\r\n1 and list 2. After data analysis with MsStats 3.5.3 I noticed elevated\r\np-values for many targets comparing to global label free proteomics results and\r\nalso to previous SRM experiment run with the same conditions but limited amount\r\nof targets that comprised in one transition list. I would like to ask you how\r\nMsStats deals with two inclusion lists. I used following dataProcess function\r\nfor normalization</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">dataProcess(raw = LCndbCDataTwoRunsList,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>                                  </span>normalization\r\n= &#39;globalStandards&#39;, nameStandards=c(&#39;x&#39;,&#39;y&#39;,&#39;z&#39;,&#39;r&#39;), </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>                                 \r\n</span>MBimpute=TRUE,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>                                 \r\n</span>censoredInt=&#39;0&#39;,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>                                 \r\n</span>cutoffCensored=&#39;minFeature&#39;,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>                                  </span>skylineReport\r\n= TRUE)</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">and following command for compatison</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">comparison&lt;-matrix(c(1,-1), nrow=1)</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">row.names(comparison)&lt;-&quot;Mut-WT&quot;</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">comparison</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">Mut.comparisons &lt;-\r\ngroupComparison(contrast.matrix = comparison,\r\ndata=LCndbCDataTwoRunsList.TMP.wStandard)</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\">I made the calculation with above\r\nfunctions for a subset of 6 proteins (3 housekeeping, 3 real targets) in two\r\nways:</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>-<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">         \r\n</span></span></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">keeping original Run assignment\r\nwhere peptides were in different transition lists so Run1 and Run2 </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"> <img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/msstats/attach/b85e94f5528a/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%201?part=0.1\" alt=\"\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span></span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>-<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">         \r\n</span></span></span><span lang=\"EN-US\">Changing all Run 2 to Run 1\r\nsimulating one transition list.</span></p><p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/msstats/attach/b85e94f5528a/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%202?part=0.2\" alt=\"\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span></span><span lang=\"EN-US\"></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">For two most\r\nimportant targets d and e the p-values are different in both calculations while\r\nthe FC looks similar. I’ve also checked condition plots and in both cases the\r\ndifference looks significant. </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\">I would like\r\nto ask you if it is a problem when the MsStat’s algorithm considers two\r\ntransition lists as technical replicates. Is condition plot enough to prove the\r\nsignificant alteration in expression?  <span><br></span></span></p><p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><br><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span></span></span></p><p style=\"margin-left:21.0pt\"><span lang=\"EN-US\"><span>Kind regards<br>Dorota<br></span></span></p>\r\n\r\n<span lang=\"EN-US\"><span> </span></span>\r\n\r\n</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "0pK-mVY-BAAJ",
   "from": "Meena Choi",
   "author": "df9ba0266a9a081f",
   "date": "2016-09-19T21:04:33Z",
   "subject": "Re: multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Dorota,<div><br></div><div>Thank you for pointing out the issue from multiple injection (different transition lists in separate injection for one sample).</div><div><br></div><div>1. We recommend not to merge two runs before normalization. need to keep separate runs.</div><div><br></div><div>2. We realized that there is the issue for summarization level in case of multiple injections. As you mentioned, it recognized as technical replicates. We will fix it.</div><div><br></div><div>3. Condition plot is just for descriptive purpose. So, Actually, not the same as significance. For data visualization and checking data itself, we recommend to check profile plot. In order to check significance with CI, recommend to see comparison plot from &#39;groupComparisonPlot&#39;.</div><div><br></div><div>Hope to help.</div><div><br></div><div>Sincerely,</div><div>- Meena</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "Ml62Es5eBAAJ",
   "from": "Dorota Muth",
   "author": "6b213090d99bc938",
   "date": "2016-09-20T06:59:31Z",
   "subject": "Re: multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Meena, <br>Thank you for your answer. I will try to normalize the runs separately and merge them later. WIll it help or should I wait until you fix this issue?<br>Regards<br>Dorota<br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "zfD8crxtBAAJ",
   "from": "Meena Choi",
   "author": "df9ba0266a9a081f",
   "date": "2016-09-20T11:33:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Dorota,<div><br></div><div>If you don&#39;t have the features that measured both Run1 and Run2, it will be alternative solution (normalize Fractionation1 and 2 separately, then merge them.)</div><div>If the data has the features measured across all runs, which seems to happen quite frequently, we can not simply change the run id. We are working on this part.</div><div><br></div><div>Sincerely,</div><div>- Meena</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "sxU8Wa_PDQAJ",
   "from": "Dorota Muth",
   "author": "6b213090d99bc938",
   "date": "2016-10-05T12:05:03Z",
   "subject": "Re: multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Meena,<br><br>I tried to run separately the normalization for both lists, combine the results later on in excel on subset of 6 proteins. When I try to run comparison on the subset of 6 proteins I get following error:<br><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:13.63636302947998px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\"> </span><span style=\"color:blue\">unique(LCndbCDataNorm.TMP.wStandard$GROUP_ORIGINAL)\r\n</span>[1] ndbC_LC WT_LC  \r\nLevels: ndbC_LC WT_LC\r\n<span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">comparison&lt;-matrix(c(1,-1), nrow=1)\r\n</span><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">row.names(comparison)&lt;-&quot;ndbC-WT&quot;\r\n</span><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">comparison\r\n</span>        [,1] [,2]\r\nndbC-WT    1   -1\r\n<span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">ndbC.comparisons &lt;- groupComparison(contrast.matrix = comparison, data=LCndbCDataNorm.TMP.wStandard)\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n Start to test and get inference in whole plot...\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Error in if (data$SummaryMethod == &quot;logOfSum&quot;) { : \r\n  argument is of length zero<br><br><br></span></pre>I wonder if it&#39;s smth wrong with the csv file that was combined after separate normalization or is there maybe some additional step to transform this csv file into suitable format for groupcomparison function. <br>The subset of data for 6 proteins combined after separate notrmalization is in the attachment.<br><br>We need to finalize the draft of manuscript including these data that&#39;s why I try to find a way to analyze these data as soon as possible. I would be greatful for any hint to make it working.<br><br>Regards<br>Dorota<br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "RUg6ZzLTDQAJ",
   "from": "Meena Choi",
   "author": "df9ba0266a9a081f",
   "date": "2016-10-05T13:09:25Z",
   "subject": "Re: multiple transition lists",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Dorota,<div><br></div><div>When you used &#39;groupComparison&#39; function,<br></div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;font-size:10pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);font-size:10pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:#000\">ndbC</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">comparisons </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> groupComparison</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">contrast</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">matrix </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> comparison</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">xxx</span></font><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></pre></div></code></div><font color=\"#0000ff\"><br></font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">the input of group comparison, here xxx, is the list format, which means that it includes some data frame and other information. So, xxx should be the output of dataProcess. So, error message asks other information.</font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">and after normalizing separately and merging together, dataProcess process next run-level summarization step. so, need to do dataProcess with merged data.</font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><br></font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Here is what I suggest.</font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">1.  I assume that &#39;ndbcvsWt_norm1C&amp;2C_6proteins.csv&#39; is normalized and merged data. Then reformat this data for MSstats input format.</font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">  <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> read</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">csv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;ndbcvsWt_norm1C&amp;2C_6proteins.csv&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><p><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp1</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></span><span style=\"color:#606\">ProteinName</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">PROTEIN</span><span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">PeptideSequence</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">PEPTIDE</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">PrecursorCharge</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">NA</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">FragmentIon</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRANSITION</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">ProductCharge</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">NA</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">IsotopeLabelType</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">LABEL</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">Condition</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">GROUP_ORIGINAL</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span></span><span style=\"color:#606\">BioReplicate</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">SUBJECT_ORIGINAL</span><span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">Run</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">originalRUN</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span></p><p><span style=\"color:#000\">                     </span><span><span style=\"color:#606\">Intensity</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">^(</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\">$</span><span><span style=\"color:#000\">ABUNDANCE</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></p></div></code></div><br></font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">2. run dataProcess without normalization</font></pre><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">quant </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dataProcess</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tmp1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normalization</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></font></div></code></div><font color=\"#000000\"><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></font></pre>3. run groupComparison<br></font><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#000\">unique</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">quant$ProcessedData</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">$GROUP_ORIGINAL</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></font></pre></div></code></div></font></pre></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font size=\"2\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre style=\"border-width:initial;border-style:none;outline:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:13.6364px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:blue\"><span style=\"color:#000\">comparison</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nrow</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></span><span style=\"color:blue\"><span style=\"color:#000\">row</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">comparison</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;ndbC-</span><span style=\"color:#080\">WT&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></span></font></pre></div></code></div></font></pre></div></div>"
    ],
    [
